s point because the one who  was fighting  now they're in the politics so they want  to rebuild afghanistan so no need to be  worried about this as i think  but can the taliban secure a new era of  trade with china and as powers shift in  the region who will reap the benefits  we have journalist reed standish from  radio free europe joining us he's been  reporting on china afghanistan relations  read there was also a recent shipment of  afghan pine nut exports to china which  the chinese ambassador made a big show  off on social media you can see here he  says it's income for afghan farmers and  a nice taste for chinese consumers  win-win  okay i mean in the grand scheme of  things though is trade between the two  countries really that important  um no i mean i think especially for  china this is a very insignificant  amount of trade that's happening on but  for afghanistan this definitely has a  big impact um and i think that this is  really being driven out of concern  coming from china but also across all  for all of afghanistan's neighbors about  you know how bad and dire the  humanitarian situation is right now in  the country um you know the taliban  don't have access to a lot of funds  they've received some aid and some  assistance but a lot of it is frozen  internationally at the moment and  china's trying to paint this narrative  you know that win-win cooperation which  we see that china says around the world  that it's stepping in and you know  filling a void um that's happening right  now in the uh in afghanistan but i think  that there's also just generally a lot  of concern across the region for how bad  things could get in the future in terms  of the humanitarian situation so china  is also trying to be uh you know  preemptive here and stave off something  that could get worse  yeah speaking of stability i want to  pivot to discuss security i assume it's  not the pine nuts china is really after  i wonder how serious is the situation  with uighur militants training in  afghanistan or is this an example of  chinese paranoia over terrorism  um i mean it's always difficult to say i  mean the  issue of weaker militants especially in  afghanistan has always been something  that's shrouded in mystery when you talk  to a lot of you know experts and  analysts who have followed these things  for decades you know they've always are  keen to put out that this is a relative  always has there's been a very small  amount of people weaker militants based  in afghanistan um in terms of the exact  number that exists right now we're not  really sure um the most official figure  that exists was a report from last year  from the u.n security council which said  that there's believed to be about you  know 500 militants in the country but of  course anything that passed through the  security council also has the input of  china so it's it's difficult to say but  of course um you know the terrorist  threat coming from the country um both  towards china and i mean towards its  neighbors is something that's very real  um and so that um is something to watch  i think moving forward  now there's been a lot of chatter as you  know after the fall of afghanistan about  a power vacuum in china's potential new  role in the region  um how much credence should we give this  well i think it's very important to say  right away you know china has no desire  to fill the vacuum left by the united  states that's not what china is going  for here um but of course there's as  we've already talked about there's a lot  of worry about how bad the situation  could get both the security situation  the humanitarian situation  um and so china is trying its best to to  wall itself off from these threats you  know it shares a small but you know not  tiny border it's about 76 kilometers i  believe with with afghanistan  and um  but you know china has no desire to have  really boots on the ground in  afghanistan or anything resembling what  we saw with the united states and even  when it comes to you know some of the  the bigger economic role that was talked  about from from china towards  afghanistan about mining rights for rare  minerals all these things you know these  are concessions that china has held for  a really long time and the fact that you  know china didn't take advantage of them  under the previous afghan government  when the security situation was much  better in the country i mean i think  that also shows that you know they're  not going to go in now so i think moving  forward the the big game for china is  you know how do we protect ourselves  from the threats in afghanistan coming  across all borders and that's really  what beijing has on its mind at the  moment  now looking more at the security  situation of course we sort of circle  back to the economic situation and the  prospects look pretty bleak  i wonder i guess we should continue to  expect big parts of afghanistan to  continue to produce opium  um i mean definitely that's been part of  the the afghan economy for for decades  and despite you know the taliban talking  about desire to to crack down on this i  mean i think that's even if there is a  genuine desire from the taliban uh being  actually being able to do this is uh you  know a very difficult task it didn't  happen in the united states um it didn't  happen under the soviet union um i  wouldn't expect it to really happen  right now under the taliban um so i  think moving forward this is going to be  part of the future of afghanistan and  it's going to continue to be a big issue  for all of afghanistan's neighbors as  well  reid standish thank you so much for  joining us  thank you
 

